    <plot x="710" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="%" y="11" xMin="0.0" height="205" legend="false" xMax="5.0" yMin="0.0" width="250" xAxis="time" display="Percent Similar">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="percent" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot percent-similar</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="325" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="% unhappy">percent-unhappy</monitor>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="260" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="num-unhappy">count turtles with [not happy?]</monitor>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="55" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="# agents">count turtles</monitor>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="120" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="% similar">percent-similar</monitor>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

This course model was adapted by Prof. Jose Magallanes from Uri Wilensky’s NetLogo Segregation model (1997). It supports reverse engineering, design critique, and experiments with a policy-relevant question.

The model explores how individual residential choices can produce collective segregation. Individuals belong to one of two groups and occupy locations on a residential grid. Each individual requires a minimum percentage of its occupied neighbors to belong to its own group. Individuals whose neighborhoods fall below that threshold are unhappy and search for an empty location.

A move changes the neighborhoods experienced by others. Their responses can generate spatial clusters even when individuals accept substantial mixing. This illustrates Schelling’s point: collective outcomes can be more segregated than individuals intended.

The model demonstrates one possible mechanism. It does not provide a complete explanation of real residential segregation or establish individuals’ motives. It does not represent housing prices, income differences, discriminatory institutions, or access to jobs and services.

## HOW TO USE IT

Click SETUP to initialize the population. Each patch has a probability of receiving one individual, controlled by DENSITY. Each new individual independently receives group 0 or group 1 with equal probability. Population size and group proportions therefore vary across runs. No patch initially contains more than one individual.

Blue and orange display group membership. Squares display happy individuals, and Xs display unhappy individuals. The `group` state determines similarity. Color displays that state, and shape displays happiness.

Click GO ONCE to execute one cycle or GO to run continuously. The world wraps across both horizontal and vertical boundaries.

DENSITY takes effect when SETUP runs. %-SIMILAR-WANTED sets the minimum percentage of same-group neighbors required for happiness. At a value of 30, an individual needs at least 30% of its occupied neighbors to belong to its own group. Run SETUP after changing the threshold when comparing fresh scenarios.

## HOW IT WORKS

* Individuals evaluate the turtles on the eight patches surrounding their current patch. Empty patches contribute no individuals to the neighbor counts.
* Each individual stores its same-group count, other-group count, total neighbor count, and happiness state.
* An individual is happy when its same-group count meets or exceeds the threshold multiplied by its total neighbor count. Under this rule, an individual with no neighbors is happy.
* GO first checks whether all individuals are happy. If they are, it stops.
* Otherwise, unhappy individuals execute `find-new-spot`. Each turns randomly and moves a random distance below ten units.
* If other individuals occupy the destination patch, `find-new-spot` calls itself again. This is recursion. Once the individual reaches an unoccupied patch, it moves to the patch center.
* The search checks occupancy, not whether the destination satisfies the individual’s threshold. Happiness is recalculated after all unhappy individuals finish moving.
* The observer then updates the collective measures and appearance, and advances CLOCK with `tick`.
* The continuous GO button repeats this sequence. The next cycle checks the updated happiness states.

## MEASURES AND VISUALIZATION

% SIMILAR reports the percentage of pooled neighbor counts that belong to the focal individual’s group. The code sums same-group neighbor counts across individuals and divides by the sum of all neighbor counts. Individuals with more neighbors contribute more weight. This is an indicator of local similarity, rather than a simple average of each individual’s percentage or a comprehensive measure of segregation.

NUM-UNHAPPY reports the number of unhappy individuals. % UNHAPPY reports that number as a percentage of the population. The Percent Similar plot displays `percent-similar`; the Number-unhappy plot displays the unhappy count.

The two groups initially mix randomly, so initial similarity will generally be near 50%, with variation between runs. Color identifies group membership. Shape makes happiness visible while students inspect the spatial pattern.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Do the clusters become more distinct as individuals move? Does a move make other individuals happy or unhappy?

Does everyone becoming happy imply that the population remains mixed? Compare the map, % SIMILAR, and % UNHAPPY before answering.

The unhappy count need not fall at every cycle. Movement can create new unhappy individuals. Outcomes and time to stop vary across parameter settings and random initial populations.

## REVERSE ENGINEERING

Recover the specification from the code before proposing improvements.

* `globals` declares model-wide measures that the observer updates.
* `turtles-own` declares individual states. Both groups use the same turtle class, rather than separate breeds.
* Patches represent A2, the residential arena. NetLogo already provides the patch grid; SETUP initializes its appearance and occupancy.
* Built-in attributes such as color, shape, size, and position need no additional declaration, but their conceptual meaning still requires specification.
* The Four As identify Agent, Arena, Artificiality, and Array. The model includes individuals, residential locations, and spatial arrangements, with no explicit housing market or institutional mechanisms.
* LUL describes classes, states, capabilities, and relations. A simple flowchart describes sequence, updates, CLOCK, and stopping conditions.

Reconfiguration may resume when inspecting implementation reveals an unclear meaning or an omitted mechanism.

## THINGS TO TRY: A POLICY QUESTION

Would greater acceptance of mixed neighborhoods be sufficient to reduce segregation?

Use BehaviorSpace to compare lower and higher values of %-SIMILAR-WANTED across several densities. Repeat each parameter combination to observe variation between runs.

A proposed classroom experiment uses densities 70, 85, and 95; thresholds 10, 30, 50, and 70; and twenty repetitions per combination, for 240 runs. Use `setup` and `go`, a limit of 1,000 steps, and final measurements of `percent-similar`, `percent-unhappy`, `ticks`, `count turtles`, and `all? turtles [ happy? ]`.

Export the results for analysis outside NetLogo. Compare similarity and satisfaction, and distinguish all-happy endings from runs reaching the limit. A capped run does not establish equilibrium.

Lowering the threshold represents a hypothesized change in preferences. It does not implement or evaluate a specific policy program. A real policy analysis must represent how an intervention changes preferences or feasible choices and use relevant empirical evidence.

## NETLOGO FEATURES AND MODEL LIMITS

`sprout` creates individuals on patches. In SETUP, each selected patch creates only one individual.

`ask` selects the individuals that act. Called procedures retain the executing individual’s context.

`find-new-spot` uses recursion and an explicit `ifelse` decision. Its base case is an unoccupied patch. `move-to patch-here` centers the individual there.

`update-turtles` calculates individual states. `update-globals` calculates collective measures. `update-appearance` displays model states.

The aggregate formulas require a nonempty population and a nonzero total neighbor count. The current code does not guard against division by zero. The movement search requires an available empty patch. A BehaviorSpace step limit does not interrupt a procedure that fails to finish within a step.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

This teaching adaptation separates group membership from color, uses an explicit `ifelse` in the recursive search, removes the visualization chooser, and separates appearance updates from state calculations. The reverse-engineering scaffold and proposed experiments are teaching adaptations, rather than claims made by the original authors.

Schelling, T. C. (1969). Models of Segregation. The American Economic Review, 59(2), 488–493.

Schelling, T. (1978). Micromotives and Macrobehavior. New York: Norton.

The 1969 reading provides the theoretical discussion. Its line example differs from this NetLogo grid implementation.

See also: Rauch, J. (2002). Seeing Around Corners. The Atlantic Monthly, April 2002, 289(4), 35–48. https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2002/04/seeing-around-corners/302471/

## HOW TO CITE

For the original model:

* Wilensky, U. (1997). NetLogo Segregation model. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/models/Segregation. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

For the software:

* Wilensky, U. (1999). NetLogo. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

When describing this version, also identify it as a teaching adaptation and explain the changes used in your analysis.

## AUTHORSHIP AND ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

Prof. Jose Magallanes prepared this teaching model for an agent-based modeling course, adapting code from Uri Wilensky’s NetLogo Segregation model (1997).

This version introduces explicit group membership, separates appearance from state calculations, and clarifies the recursive movement procedure. It includes a reverse-engineering teaching approach and a proposed BehaviorSpace policy experiment.

The model draws on Thomas Schelling’s work on how individual residential preferences can generate collective segregation.

This course version is distinct from the model distributed in the NetLogo Models Library. The original code attribution and license remain below.

## COPYRIGHT AND LICENSE

Copyright 1997 Uri Wilensky.

